=== Final PC location, identified ===
pc=0x1003157c -> SHA1_Update (software/upstream/doomgeneric/sha1.c)
Other late-run PCs (0x10030020/0x10030fa8/0x1002fdc4) -> Transform (sha1.c's
internal SHA1 block-compression function)

Traced the call path: d_net.c:204 calls W_Checksum(connect_data->wad_sha1sum)
(part of vanilla DOOM's always-present network-game-info setup, even for
single player -- this project's own scope decision #9 already noted
d_net.c is compiled but unused for actual networking). W_Checksum
(software/upstream/doomgeneric/w_checksum.c) iterates over EVERY entry in
lumpinfo[] -- all 3,163 real lumps, now that the directory is correctly
populated -- calling ChecksumAddLump() for each, which feeds the lump's
NAME, file number, position, and size (all METADATA already present from
the preloaded directory -- NOT lump content) into a running SHA1 hash via
SHA1_UpdateString/SHA1_UpdateInt32, each occasionally triggering
Transform() when SHA1's internal 64-byte block buffer fills.

This is real, correct, bounded (O(3163)) engine code -- not a hang, not
an unexpected code path -- computing the loaded WAD's identity hash as
part of DOOM's standard startup sequence. It explains why cycles
~50,000,000-60,000,000 were spent in a tight rotation through a handful
of addresses (Transform/SHA1_Update/M_StringCopy): a per-lump loop with
several hash-update calls each, over thousands of lumps, on a CPU where
every memory access costs several cycles via the close-page SDRAM
controller.
